edb/ref 17592186298048} {:answer/id #uuid "a883a99c-14d4-570b-9be2-b865e1ef76ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=-\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298289} {:answer/id #uuid "ba45b0a6-2daf-56d9-b16a-965870ed06a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298363}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "26ece22b-5acf-5c52-8db3-6eda259a35ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "is not  exact" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297713} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4f6459e-227f-5366-8cf8-402d1d082fa2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299462}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a4505d16-2cca-5cfc-8b1c-8092ab2f2f00" :question/math-academy-id "q-320450" :question/problem "Determine whether the following differential equation is exact:\n\n$$\n(4+{y}^{2}{e}^{xy})\\,\\text{d}x+(x{e}^{xy}+3y)\\,\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nA differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nis exact if it satisfies the condition\n\n$$\n\\,{{field-1}},\n$$\n\nFor the equation $(4+{y}^{2}{e}^{xy})\\,\\text{d}x+(x{e}^{xy}+3y)\\,\\text{d}y=0$, we have  \n\n$$\nP=\\,{{field-2}},\n$$\n\n$$\nQ=\\,{{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\,{{field-4}},\n$$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\,{{field-5}}.\n$$\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n\\,{{field-6}},\n$$\n\n the equation {{field-7}}." :question/worked-solution "A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nFor the equation $(4+{y}^{2}{e}^{xy})\\,\\text{d}x+(x{e}^{xy}+3y)\\,\\text{d}y=0$, we have\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=4+{y}^{2}{e}^{xy},\\,Q(x,y)=x{e}^{xy}+3y.\n$$\n\nNow, compute the partial derivatives carefully:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\,(4+{y}^{2}{e}^{xy}) \\\\  & =2y{e}^{xy}+x{y}^{2}{e}^{xy}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\,(x{e}^{xy}+3y) \\\\  & ={e}^{xy}+xy{e}^{xy}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}\\ne \\frac{∂Q}{∂x},\n$$\n\nso the exactness condition fails. Therefore, the given differential equation $\\text{is not exact}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299861 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d45fb1d-4aba-5e5b-8d1b-5877ead0d0a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{4}{y}^{4}}{4}-\\frac{13}{2}{x}^{2}+22x=c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297677} {:answer/id #uuid "423c78ad-da61-5ab6-a21e-00c8efac2fcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{{x}^{4}{y}^{4}}{4}-\\frac{13}{2}{y}^{2}+22x=c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297819} {:answer/id #uuid "544d8fb4-ba70-550d-99e6-b16a3076e838" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{4}{y}^{4}}{4}+\\frac{13}{2}{y}^{2}-22x=c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297909} {:answer/id #uuid "692d22a8-b7e5-5ffe-9590-1e47a369c859" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{4}{y}^{4}}{4}-\\frac{13}{2}{y}^{2}+22y=c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298003} {:answer/id #uuid "ed03663f-5ad8-5aaa-9cdf-b319136088da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{4}{y}^{4}}{4}-\\frac{13}{2}{y}^{2}+22x=c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298561}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ed03663f-5ad8-5aaa-9cdf-b319136088da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{4}{y}^{4}}{4}-\\frac{13}{2}{y}^{2}+22x=c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298561} :answer-field/id #uuid "05b10473-d463-5cef-b2e1-b37d7eb3aaa0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298657}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9285c033-5bae-5080-b27a-b8c40b5b8da1" :question/math-academy-id "q-320578" :question/problem "Find the general solution of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{22+{x}^{3}{y}^{4}}{\\,13y-{x}^{4}{y}^{3}\\,}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We start by rewriting this equation in differential form.\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nStarting from \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{22+{x}^{3}{y}^{4}}{13y-{x}^{4}{y}^{3}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe first multiply through by $(13y-{x}^{4}{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x$:\n\n$$\n(13y-{x}^{4}{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}y=(22+{x}^{3}{y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThen, we rearrange the terms:\n\n$$\n(22+{x}^{3}{y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}x+(22+{x}^{3}{y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nNow, we should check for exactness. A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nFor our differential equation, we have\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=22+{x}^{3}{y}^{4},\\,Q(x,y)={x}^{4}{y}^{3}-13y.\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\,(22+{x}^{3}{y}^{4}) \\\\  & =\\,4{x}^{3}{y}^{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\,(x\\,(xy)^{3}-13y) \\\\  & =\\,4{x}^{3}{y}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\\,✓\n$$\n\n \nThus, our equation is exact.\n\nSince the equation is exact, our solution $u(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂u}{∂x}=P(x,y),\\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y}=Q(x,y)\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $P(x,y)$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int P(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+f(y) \\\\  & =\\int (22+{x}^{3}{y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}x+f(y) \\\\  & =22x+\\frac{{x}^{4}{y}^{4}}{4}+f(y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate $Q(x,y)$ with respect to $y.$  Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+g(x) \\\\  & =\\int ({x}^{4}{y}^{3}-13y)\\,\\text{d}y+g(x) \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{4}{y}^{4}}{4}-\\frac{13}{2}{y}^{2}+g(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the two solutions, we see that we must have\n\n$$\ng(x)=22x,\\,f(y)=-\\frac{13}{2}{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution $u(x,y)=c$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{4}{y}^{4}}{4}-\\frac{13}{2}{y}^{2}+22x=c.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299862 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4470b9d3-f3cf-508a-a89e-4ef62fd28cd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-{y}^{2}+3x=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297832} {:answer/id #uuid "a53c9892-7118-5c02-9994-4d4e802ea404" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({y}^{2}-3x)\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298277} {:answer/id #uuid "ad87cc2e-adc3-5fff-8e27-af54217ecd9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)-3x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1)-1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298308} {:answer/id #uuid "e9eb6e83-3c3e-5ae4-b75c-7d6c5c8d0743" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-3x=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298548} {:answer/id #uuid "f416b2b5-798f-59e2-b099-f93bac9564cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+{y}^{2}-3x=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298589}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4470b9d3-f3cf-508a-a89e-4ef62fd28cd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-{y}^{2}+3x=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297832} :answer-field/id #uuid "c13dc231-1f5a-5ef2-b37b-e3fb02d05e69" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299286}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5bdd84df-d1f6-5328-b5af-cb71bdcbb4a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-320585" :question/problem "Find the general solution of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+3}{\\,2y-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\,},\n$$\n\nwith the initial condition $y(0)=1.$" :question/worked-solution "We start by rewriting this equation in differential form.\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nStarting from \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+3}{\\,2y-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\,},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe first multiply through by$(2y-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy))\\,\\text{d}x$:\n\n$$\n(2y-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy))\\,\\text{d}y=(y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+3)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThen, we rearrange the terms:\n\n$$\n(y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+3)\\,\\text{d}x+(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)-2y)\\,\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nNow, we should check for exactness. A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nFor our differential equation, we have\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+3,\\,Q(x,y)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)-2y.\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+3) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)-2y) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\\,✓\n$$\n\nThus, our equation is exact.\n\nSince the equation is exact, our solution $u(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂u}{∂x}=P(x,y),\\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y}=Q(x,y)\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $P(x,y)$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int P(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+f(y) \\\\  & =\\int (y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+3)\\,\\text{d}x+f(y) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+3x+f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate $Q(x,y)$ with respect to $y.$  Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+g(x) \\\\  & =\\int (x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)-2y)\\,\\text{d}y+g(x) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-{y}^{2}+g(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the two solutions, we see that we must have\n\n$$\ng(x)=3x,\\,f(y)=-{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution $u(x,y)=c$ is\n\n$$\n-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-{y}^{2}+3x=c.\n$$\n\nThe particular solution specified by the initial condition must have $y=1$  when $x=0.$ These values determine the value of the constant $c.$ Substituting this into the equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-{y}^{2}+3x & =c \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0\\cdot 1)-(1)^{2}+3\\cdot 0 & =c \\\\ c & =-1-1 \\\\ c & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-{y}^{2}+3x=-2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299863 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17e732f3-9a70-5de6-aeb7-d3b1945a436a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)-2{y}^{2}+5x=-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297654} {:answer/id #uuid "1f71976d-db92-564f-b611-956bd07ce84a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)-5x=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297688} {:answer/id #uuid "33e82307-3bc9-55fb-be40-94942eb29aee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({y}^{2}-5x)\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297761} {:answer/id #uuid "40702e8c-18c0-597d-b1a2-55f18934de47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-5x=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297810} {:answer/id #uuid "d91863d8-69a3-5aba-8a08-db175102daed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+2{y}^{2}+5x=8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298484}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "17e732f3-9a70-5de6-aeb7-d3b1945a436a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)-2{y}^{2}+5x=-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297654} :answer-field/id #uuid "19e9673c-4294-5f40-a3c9-fe9684eb341e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298730}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f17832a3-73ac-531a-b06a-a29c9fd5eb32" :question/math-academy-id "q-320586" :question/problem "Find the general solution of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+5}{\\,4y-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)\\,},\n$$\n\nwith the initial condition $y(0)=-2.$" :question/worked-solution "We start by rewriting this equation in differential form.\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nStarting from \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+5}{4y-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe first multiply through by $(4y-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy))\\,\\text{d}x$:\n\n$$\n(4y-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy))\\,\\text{d}y=(y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+5)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThen, we rearrange the terms:\n\n$$\n(y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+5)\\,\\text{d}x+(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-4y)\\,\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nNow, we should check for exactness. A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nFor our differential equation, we have\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+5,\\,Q(x,y)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-4y.\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+5) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-xy\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-4y) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-xy\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\\,✓\n$$\n\nThus, our equation is exact.\n\nSince the equation is exact, our solution $u(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂u}{∂x}=P(x,y),\\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y}=Q(x,y)\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $P(x,y)$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int P(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+f(y) \\\\  & =\\int (y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+5)\\,\\text{d}x+f(y) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+5x+f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate $Q(x,y)$ with respect to $y.$  Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+g(x) \\\\  & =\\int (x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-4y)\\,\\text{d}y+g(x) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)-2{y}^{2}+g(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the two solutions, we see that we must have\n\n$$\ng(x)=5x,\\,f(y)=-2{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution $u(x,y)=c$ is\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)-2{y}^{2}+5x=c.\n$$\n\nThe particular solution specified by the initial condition must have $y=-2$ when $x=0.$ These values determine the value of the constant $c.$ Substituting this into the equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)-2{y}^{2}+5x & =c \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(0\\cdot (-2))-2(-2)^{2}+5\\cdot 0 & =c \\\\ c & =0-8 \\\\ c & =-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)-2{y}^{2}+5x=-8.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299864 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3558038e-fdaf-574f-86e2-7d628cebeea7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}+{e}^{xy}-5x=17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297765} {:answer/id #uuid "4b508b7e-b421-512d-8f78-61b2ed419320" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{xy}-2{y}^{2}-5x=-31" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297860} {:answer/id #uuid "60901254-8035-53af-985d-aacb45400bf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({y}^{2}+5x)\\,{e}^{xy}=16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297960} {:answer/id #uuid "7048e8cf-3758-5ee1-9b9f-e8365669c21b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{xy}+2{y}^{2}+5x=33" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298032} {:answer/id #uuid "afff133c-08da-59aa-bdd8-00f0e2a5bf70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}-{e}^{xy}+5x=15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298319}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4b508b7e-b421-512d-8f78-61b2ed419320" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{xy}-2{y}^{2}-5x=-31" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297860} :answer-field/id #uuid "93023fb5-7a54-5007-9913-cf50ede89eca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299123}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f2cb23bd-6a0e-55a8-8740-0231e5c31fd5" :question/math-academy-id "q-320589" :question/problem "Find the general solution of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{y{e}^{xy}-5}{\\,4y-x{e}^{xy}\\,},\n$$\n\nwith the initial condition $y(0)=4.$" :question/worked-solution "We start by rewriting this equation in differential form.\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nStarting from \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{y{e}^{xy}-5}{4y-x{e}^{xy}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe first multiply through by $(4y-x{e}^{xy})\\,\\text{d}x$:\n\n$$\n(4y-x{e}^{xy})\\,\\text{d}y=(y{e}^{xy}-5)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThen, we rearrange the terms:\n\n$$\n(y{e}^{xy}-5)\\,\\text{d}x+(x{e}^{xy}-4y)\\,\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nNow, we should check for exactness. A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nFor our differential equation, we have\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=y{e}^{xy}-5,\\,Q(x,y)=x{e}^{xy}-4y.\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y{e}^{xy}-5) \\\\  & ={e}^{xy}+xy{e}^{xy}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{e}^{xy}-4y) \\\\  & ={e}^{xy}+xy{e}^{xy}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\\,✓\n$$\n\nThus, our equation is exact.\n\nSince the equation is exact, our solution $u(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂u}{∂x}=P(x,y),\\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y}=Q(x,y)\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $P(x,y)$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int P(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+f(y) \\\\  & =\\int (y{e}^{xy}-5)\\,\\text{d}x+f(y) \\\\  & ={e}^{xy}-5x+f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate $Q(x,y)$ with respect to $y.$  Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+g(x) \\\\  & =\\int (x{e}^{xy}-4y)\\,\\text{d}y+g(x) \\\\  & ={e}^{xy}-2{y}^{2}+g(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the two solutions, we see that we must have\n\n$$\ng(x)=-5x,\\,f(y)=-2{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution $u(x,y)=c$ is\n\n$$\n{e}^{xy}-2{y}^{2}-5x=c.\n$$\n\nThe particular solution specified by the initial condition must have $y=4$ when $x=0.$ These values determine the value of the constant $c.$ Substituting this into the equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{xy}-2{y}^{2}-5x & =c \\\\ {e}^{0\\cdot 4}-2(4)^{2}-5\\cdot 0 & =c \\\\ c & =1-32 \\\\ c & =-31\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n{e}^{xy}-2{y}^{2}-5x=-31.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299906 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d2303e32-8b26-55ec-9d0f-18e1bc3899cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{3}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}(3){log}_{3}(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298462}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d2303e32-8b26-55ec-9d0f-18e1bc3899cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{3}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}(3){log}_{3}(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298462} :answer-field/id #uuid "792dd6e3-1db8-586c-a5f7-0da2170dac92" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299050}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d825ca35-2305-5442-afb6-a99517062893" :question/math-academy-id "q-320814" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={3}^{x}{log}_{3}⁡(x),$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}} $+\\,\\frac{{3}^{x}}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of the two functions $u(x)={3}^{x}$ and $v(x)={log}_{3}⁡(x).$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x) \\\\  & ={({3}^{x})}^{′}{log}_{3}⁡(x)+{3}^{x}{({log}_{3}⁡(x))}^{′} \\\\  & =({3}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3))({log}_{3}⁡(x))+({3}^{x})\\left(\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}\\right) \\\\  & ={3}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3){log}_{3}⁡(x)+\\frac{{3}^{x}}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299907 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ca46b1c5-ece7-5119-abe8-777dcc7ec3b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{4}^{x}}{x\\operatorname{ln}(4)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298433}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ca46b1c5-ece7-5119-abe8-777dcc7ec3b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{4}^{x}}{x\\operatorname{ln}(4)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298433} :answer-field/id #uuid "0aa44b57-7202-5d5a-9c5f-cd88604e7d86" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298669}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6161762d-7ca8-5487-95de-c569a1e07882" :question/math-academy-id "q-320815" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={4}^{x}{log}_{4}⁡(x),$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)={4}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4){log}_{4}⁡(x)\\,+$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of the two functions $u(x)={4}^{x}$ and $v(x)={log}_{4}⁡(x).$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x) \\\\  & ={({4}^{x})}^{′}{log}_{4}⁡(x)+{4}^{x}{({log}_{4}⁡(x))}^{′} \\\\  & =({4}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4))({log}_{4}⁡(x))+({4}^{x})\\left(\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}\\right) \\\\  & ={4}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4){log}_{4}⁡(x)+\\frac{{4}^{x}}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299908 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e34c232-975f-51cf-8ed5-ecf0f78eff8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{5}^{-x}\\operatorname{ln}(5){log}_{7}(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297797} {:answer/id #uuid "8a591582-6e6f-4147-9d4d-ddb81a77153c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5^{-x}\\ln5\\left(\\log_7x\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186576151}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3e34c232-975f-51cf-8ed5-ecf0f78eff8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{5}^{-x}\\operatorname{ln}(5){log}_{7}(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297797} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b389b0c-f96f-5746-b4d1-da5e3f5fb10c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298838}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "42b535b3-90b5-5bc5-bbb3-36d4082e369c" :question/math-academy-id "q-320816" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={5}^{-x}{log}_{7}⁡(x),$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}} $+\\,\\frac{{5}^{-x}}{xln⁡(7)}$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of the two functions $u(x)={5}^{-x}$ and $v(x)={log}_{7}⁡(x).$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x) \\\\  & ={({5}^{-x})}^{′}{log}_{7}⁡(x)+{5}^{-x}{({log}_{7}⁡(x))}^{′} \\\\  & =(-{5}^{-x}ln⁡(5))({log}_{7}⁡(x))+({5}^{-x})\\left(\\frac{1}{xln⁡(7)}\\right) \\\\  & =-{5}^{-x}ln⁡(5){log}_{7}⁡(x)+\\frac{{5}^{-x}}{xln⁡(7)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299930 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "343a96f6-3b3f-5d74-8efe-270552aea7b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{xy}-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}+3x=\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297762} {:answer/id #uuid "63866727-37e6-5423-b4a9-ddf0be7e5e05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({y}^{2}+3x){e}^{xy}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297977} {:answer/id #uuid "6faf6a70-5ef1-5725-bd9d-af341ba97ead" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{e}^{xy}-\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{2}+3x=\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298027} {:answer/id #uuid "77e6e1d1-a7f7-5b37-b27f-4b2987359b34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}+{e}^{xy}+3x=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298075} {:answer/id #uuid "be381c5e-2e85-5917-87c1-7b5cd91b6802" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{e}^{xy}+\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{2}+3x=\\frac{11}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298386}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6faf6a70-5ef1-5725-bd9d-af341ba97ead" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{e}^{xy}-\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{2}+3x=\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298027} :answer-field/id #uuid "a4a27961-ba48-52e9-b624-1b43f37e601d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299191}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5fa18d3e-44c1-5bdd-8073-b5de69417d5e" :question/math-academy-id "q-321147" :question/problem "Find the general solution of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{3+4y\\,{e}^{xy}}{3y-4x\\,{e}^{xy}},\n$$\n\nwith the initial condition $y(0)=1.$" :question/worked-solution "We start by rewriting this equation in differential form.\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nStarting from \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{3+4y\\,{e}^{xy}}{3y-4x\\,{e}^{xy}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe first multiply through by $(3y-4x\\,{e}^{xy})\\,\\text{d}x$:\n\n$$\n(3y-4x\\,{e}^{xy})\\,\\text{d}y=(3+4y\\,{e}^{xy})\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThen, we rearrange the terms:\n\n$$\n(3+4y\\,{e}^{xy})\\,\\text{d}x+(4x\\,{e}^{xy}-3y)\\,\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nNow, we should check for exactness. A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nFor our differential equation, we have\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=3+4y\\,{e}^{xy},\\,Q(x,y)=4x\\,{e}^{xy}-3y.\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3+4y\\,{e}^{xy}) \\\\  & =4{e}^{xy}+4xy\\,{e}^{xy}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4x\\,{e}^{xy}-3y) \\\\  & =4{e}^{xy}+4xy\\,{e}^{xy}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\\,✓\n$$\n\n \nThus, our equation is exact.\n\nSince the equation is exact, our solution $u(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂u}{∂x}=P(x,y),\\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y}=Q(x,y)\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $P(x,y)$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int P(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+f(y) \\\\  & =\\int (3+4y\\,{e}^{xy})\\,\\text{d}x+f(y) \\\\  & =3x+4{e}^{xy}+f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate $Q(x,y)$ with respect to $y.$  Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+g(x) \\\\  & =\\int (4x\\,{e}^{xy}-3y)\\,\\text{d}y+g(x) \\\\  & =4{e}^{xy}-\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{2}+g(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the two solutions, we see that we must have\n\n$$\ng(x)=3x,\\,f(y)=-\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution $u(x,y)=c$ is\n\n$$\n4{e}^{xy}-\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{2}+3x=c.\n$$\n\nThe particular solution specified by the initial condition must have $y=1$ when $x=0.$ These values determine the value of the constant $c.$ Substituting this into the equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{e}^{xy}-\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{2}+3x & =c \\\\ 4{e}^{0\\cdot 1}-\\frac{3}{2}(1)^{2}+3\\cdot 0 & =c \\\\ c & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n4{e}^{xy}-\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{2}+3x=\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299931 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23aeecf1-4b0c-502b-a770-03fb710bf98a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{5}{y}^{5}}{5}-{x}^{2}+2y=c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297704} {:answer/id #uuid "47792620-3a56-571f-89d5-96a62de8fafe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{5}{y}^{5}}{5}-2x+2y=c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297845} {:answer/id #uuid "78fe55b2-43c5-529e-80de-f6e64e2323f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{5}{y}^{5}}{5}-{y}^{2}+2x=c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298080} {:answer/id #uuid "807b9e5b-4e6c-5db8-8139-9e799e09d909" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{5}{y}^{5}}{5}-{x}^{2}+2x=c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298106} {:answer/id #uuid "86b62e8a-bf01-5dfb-993e-7b88699ffca4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{5}{y}^{5}}{5}-x+2y=c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298133}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "78fe55b2-43c5-529e-80de-f6e64e2323f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{5}{y}^{5}}{5}-{y}^{2}+2x=c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298080} :answer-field/id #uuid "88f7eacd-5399-5294-baac-683660594e6f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299097}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5168ce0a-8a11-5366-8599-b3a807752143" :question/math-academy-id "q-321203" :question/problem "Find the general solution of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{2+{x}^{4}{y}^{5}}{2y-{x}^{5}{y}^{4}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We start by rewriting this equation in differential form.\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nStarting from \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2+{x}^{4}{y}^{5}}{2y-{x}^{5}{y}^{4}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe first multiply through by $(2y-{x}^{5}{y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}x$:\n\n$$\n(2y-{x}^{5}{y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}y=(2+{x}^{4}{y}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThen, we  rearrange the terms:\n\n$$\n(2+{x}^{4}{y}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x+({x}^{5}{y}^{4}-2y)\\,\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nNow, we should check for exactness. A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nFor our differential equation, we have\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=2+{x}^{4}{y}^{5},\\,Q(x,y)={x}^{5}{y}^{4}-2y.\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2+{x}^{4}{y}^{5}) \\\\  & =5{x}^{4}{y}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{5}{y}^{4}-2y) \\\\  & =5{x}^{4}{y}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\\,✓\n$$\n\nThus, our equation is exact.\n\nSince the equation is exact, our solution $u(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂u}{∂x}=P(x,y),\\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y}=Q(x,y)\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $P(x,y)$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int P(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+f(y) \\\\  & =\\int (2+{x}^{4}{y}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x+f(y) \\\\  & =2x+\\frac{{x}^{5}{y}^{5}}{5}+f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate $Q(x,y)$ with respect to $y.$  Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+g(x) \\\\  & =\\int ({x}^{5}{y}^{4}-2y)\\,\\text{d}y+g(x) \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{5}{y}^{5}}{5}-{y}^{2}+g(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the two solutions, we see that we must have\n\n$$\ng(x)=2x,\\,f(y)=-{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution $u(x,y)=c$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{5}{y}^{5}}{5}-{y}^{2}+2x=c.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319183 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30f2aa3c-8d6c-58b6-8fad-021601d8fb4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317717}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "30f2aa3c-8d6c-58b6-8fad-021601d8fb4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317717} :answer-field/id #uuid "780aeb43-18e5-575c-b4c1-ed0023b8cb41" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318677}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "71180eab-0a82-50eb-a6d2-bb1fd471ef0c" :question/math-academy-id "q-347510" :question/problem "Aluminum-26 has a half-life of $0.717$ million years. A sample of aluminum-26 has an initial mass of $40\\,\\mu \\text{g}.$ Rounded to **one decimal place**, what is the mass of the sample after $1$ million years?\n\nThe mass of the sample after $1$ million years is approximately\n{{field-1}}\n$\\mu \\text{g}.$" :question/worked-solution "The mass $M(t)$ of a radioactive substance at time $t$ can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n{M}^{′}(t)=-kM(t),\\,M(0)={M}_{0}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-kt}.\n$$\n\nThe half-life ${t}_{1/2}$ is the time taken for the mass of the substance to reduce by one half and is given by\n\n$$\n{t}_{1/2}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}.\n$$\n\nSince we know that ${t}_{1/2}=0.717$ million years, we have\n\n$$\n0.717=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}\\,⇒\\,k=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{0.717}.\n$$\n\nAlso, since ${M}_{0}=40\\,\\mu \\text{g},$ we have the particular solution\n\n$$\nM(t)=40{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/0.717}.\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nM(1)=40{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/0.717}≈15.2,\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the mass of the sample remaining after $1$ million years is approximately $15.2\\,\\mu \\text{g}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319344 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f193d193-1bf0-5987-8095-5e68075aa685" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{124}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318367}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f193d193-1bf0-5987-8095-5e68075aa685" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{124}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318367} :answer-field/id #uuid "cdbd4d07-bfd0-51cd-a3e5-380d16ff0679" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318863}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d36c1c11-19b9-5940-860c-a11b298fedf7" :question/math-academy-id "q-349595" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}4{e}^{5x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$  \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If we let $u=5x,$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=5\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{5}\\text{d}u=\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nComputing our limits of integration, we get the following:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\operatorname{ln}⁡2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $5\\operatorname{ln}⁡2$ |\n\nThen, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}4{e}^{5x}\\,\\text{d}x & =4{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{e}^{5x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{5\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{e}^{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({2}^{5})}{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}\\cdot {e}^{u}\\,{|}_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({2}^{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}({e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({2}^{5})}-{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}({2}^{5}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}(32-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}\\cdot 31 \\\\  & =\\frac{124}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319345 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3edf67a7-98ed-5fa7-a584-a4df28da0c03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "105" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317765}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3edf67a7-98ed-5fa7-a584-a4df28da0c03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "105" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317765} :answer-field/id #uuid "96b04790-7dd5-55c7-ad74-3f32de8c4fb5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318758}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b9d82eda-7286-547a-83ef-0ce6d0452b88" :question/math-academy-id "q-349596" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}5{e}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$  \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If we let $u=3x,$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u=\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nComputing our limits of integration, we get the following:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\operatorname{ln}⁡4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $3\\operatorname{ln}⁡4$ |\n\nThen, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}5{e}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x & =5{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}{e}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5{\\int }_{0}^{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}{e}^{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({4}^{3})}{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}\\cdot {e}^{u}\\,{|}_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({4}^{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}({e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({4}^{3})}-{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}({4}^{3}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}(64-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}(63) \\\\  & =105\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319346 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a2699f10-0b6b-5d35-aaa2-36b73e978859" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{9}-e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318120}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a2699f10-0b6b-5d35-aaa2-36b73e978859" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{9}-e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318120} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e8ce480-0b94-536b-9ea9-402ad657f2e0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318740}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8852114f-8bc0-5518-9880-52e4b44b23ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-349601" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{2}4{e}^{4x+1}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$  \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If we let $u=4x+1,$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=4\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{4}\\text{d}u=\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nComputing our limits of integration, we get the following:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $9$ |\n\nThen, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}4{e}^{4x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =4{\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{4x+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{1}^{9}{e}^{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{9}{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={e}^{u}\\,{|}_{1}^{9} \\\\  & ={e}^{9}-{e}^{1} \\\\  & ={e}^{9}-e\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319347 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f9887105-989f-5006-a00d-02997b258f61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{108}{\\operatorname{ln}3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318395} {:answer/id #uuid "0adf2cfd-fb85-4161-8d4d-8bc7b662df6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{108}{\\ln3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186676211}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f9887105-989f-5006-a00d-02997b258f61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{108}{\\operatorname{ln}3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318395} :answer-field/id #uuid "a4926046-d89b-5f97-a176-004edee262b6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318786}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "94f9e06c-ebba-5efe-8b0d-89981a71a1e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-349625" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{2}{3}^{7-2x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$  \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If we let $u=7-2x,$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-2\\,⟹\\,-\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u=\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nComputing our limits of integration, we get the following:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $5$ | $3$ |\n\nThen, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{3}^{7-2x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{5}^{3}{3}^{u}\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{5}^{3}{3}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\frac{{3}^{u}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\right){|}_{5}^{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}({3}^{3}-{3}^{5}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}(27-243) \\\\  & =\\frac{108}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319348 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cabb7ef7-a5b4-56a3-899b-b57c3c0202b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{36}{\\operatorname{ln}2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318237}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cabb7ef7-a5b4-56a3-899b-b57c3c0202b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{36}{\\operatorname{ln}2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318237} :answer-field/id #uuid "b60ea954-3843-5ca8-a4a4-8f56cec4f3ce" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318818}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "84ff8262-4ace-5df5-9e7e-b8458e5e65ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-349626" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{3}3\\cdot {2}^{5-x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$  \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If we let $u=5-x,$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-1\\,⟹\\,-\\,\\text{d}u=\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nComputing our limits of integration, we get the following:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $4$ | $2$ |\n\nThen, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{3}3\\cdot {2}^{5-x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{4}^{2}3\\cdot {2}^{u}(-1)\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-3{\\int }_{4}^{2}{2}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-3\\left(\\frac{{2}^{u}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\right){|}_{4}^{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}({2}^{2}-{2}^{4}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(4-16) \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319349 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c1e0d6d6-2adc-5995-80ee-179a5a470810" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{\\operatorname{ln}4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318207}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c1e0d6d6-2adc-5995-80ee-179a5a470810" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{\\operatorname{ln}4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318207} :answer-field/id #uuid "7afa991a-6722-56e2-897c-813ea6c1aef9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318684}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "efb81182-b47b-5e4a-b191-d85df24ba0ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-349631" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1}2\\cdot {4}^{1-x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$  \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If we let $u=1-x,$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-1\\,⟹\\,-\\,\\text{d}u=\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nComputing our limits of integration, we get the following:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n\nThen, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}2\\cdot {4}^{1-x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{0}2\\cdot {4}^{u}(-1)\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-2{\\int }_{1}^{0}{4}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-2\\left(\\frac{{4}^{u}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\right){|}_{1}^{0} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}({4}^{0}-{4}^{1}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}(1-4) \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319350 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "723152d6-ad7a-5796-b242-18a007f91b9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{24}{5\\operatorname{ln}5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317958}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "723152d6-ad7a-5796-b242-18a007f91b9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{24}{5\\operatorname{ln}5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317958} :answer-field/id #uuid "58ca831c-f339-532f-9ae7-145293aca3d4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318616}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "442a9420-19fe-5c7f-9765-ddb48edbec27" :question/math-academy-id "q-349646" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{2}{5}^{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$  \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If we let $u=x-1,$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nComputing our limits of integration, we get the following:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $-1$ | $1$ |\n\nThen, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{5}^{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{5}^{u}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{{5}^{u}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}\\,{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}({5}^{1}-{5}^{-1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}(5-\\frac{1}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{24}{5\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319351 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fd67e3a7-28e0-5d80-ab9d-4eee46bad316" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{\\operatorname{ln}3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318412} {:answer/id #uuid "3e3d87a9-87ed-4357-b5aa-1afa761e9446" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{\\ln\\left(3\\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186676213}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fd67e3a7-28e0-5d80-ab9d-4eee46bad316" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{\\operatorname{ln}3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318412} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f378587-0bf1-5acc-baa3-21eda4addc7b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318771}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5ff1abc0-7cbc-5bd4-915a-56c8cc58e75d" :question/math-academy-id "q-349648" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-1}^{1}{3}^{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$  \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If we let $u=x+1,$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nComputing our limits of integration, we get the following:\n\n| $x$ | $-1$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $2$ |\n\nThen, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{3}^{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{3}^{u}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{{3}^{u}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\,{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}({3}^{2}-{3}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}(9-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321132 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34d7b18f-cfd0-5ed7-bce2-b694d3550c3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319555}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "34d7b18f-cfd0-5ed7-bce2-b694d3550c3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319555} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe1d4079-dd7c-5853-8e2a-eee8d10b4947" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320708}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "30e29f26-7f67-5a4d-9775-f9729308f554" :question/math-academy-id "q-353464" :question/problem "Iodine-131 has a half-life of $8.02$ days. A sample of iodine-131 has an initial mass of $7.2\\,\\mu \\text{g}.$ Rounded to **one decimal place**, what is the mass of the sample after $12$ days?\n\nThe mass of the sample after $12$ days is approximately\n{{field-1}}\n$\\mu \\text{g}.$" :question/worked-solution "The mass $M(t)$ of a radioactive substance at time $t$ can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n{M}^{′}(t)=-kM(t),\\,M(0)={M}_{0}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-kt}.\n$$\n\nThe half-life ${t}_{1/2}$ is the time taken for the mass of the substance to reduce by one half and is given by\n\n$$\n{t}_{1/2}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}.\n$$\n\nSince we know that ${t}_{1/2}=8.02$ days, we have\n\n$$\n8.02=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}\\,⇒\\,k=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{8.02}.\n$$\n\nAlso, since ${M}_{0}=7.2\\,\\mu \\text{g},$ we have the particular solution\n\n$$\nM(t)=7.2{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/8.02}.\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nM(12)=7.2{e}^{-12\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/8.02}≈2.6,\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the mass of the sample remaining after $12$ days is approximately $2.6\\,\\mu \\text{g}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321138 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5d19fd66-2731-5e3e-9dd2-9505457e34e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21.08" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319669}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5d19fd66-2731-5e3e-9dd2-9505457e34e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21.08" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319669} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b975067-7f73-5968-a997-f1dc926431ac" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320389}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d92e703c-8485-513b-8983-fd7fd22219c5" :question/math-academy-id "q-353520" :question/problem "The half-life of cobalt-60 is $5.27$ years. How long does it take for a $8.0\\,\\mu \\text{g}$ sample of cobalt-60 to reduce to $0.5\\,\\mu \\text{g}?$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$t=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{yr}$" :question/worked-solution "The mass $M(t)$ of a radioactive substance at time $t$ can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n{M}^{′}(t)=-kM(t),\\,M(0)={M}_{0}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-kt}.\n$$\n\nThe half-life ${t}_{1/2}$ is the time taken for the mass of the substance to reduce by one half and is given by\n\n$$\n{t}_{1/2}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}.\n$$\n\nThere are two methods that we can use to solve this problem. We describe each of them below.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nSince we know that ${t}_{1/2}=5.27$ years, we have\n\n$$\n5.27=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}\\,⇒\\,k=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{5.27}\n$$\n\nAlso, since ${M}_{0}=8.0\\,\\mu \\text{g},$ we have the particular solution\n\n$$\nM(t)=8.0{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/5.27}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to calculate the time $t$ such that $M(t)=0.5\\,\\mu \\text{g}.$ So, we set up our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.5 & =8.0{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/5.27}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.5 & =8.0{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/5.27} \\\\ \\frac{0.5}{8.0} & ={e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/5.27} \\\\ \\frac{1}{16} & ={e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/5.27} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{16}\\right) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/5.27}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡16 & =-\\frac{t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{5.27} \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡16 & =-\\frac{t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{5.27} \\\\ t & =\\frac{5.27\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡16}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ t & =\\frac{5.27\\cdot 4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ t & =21.08\\,\\text{yr}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe wish to calculate the time $t$ such that $M(t)=0.5\\,\\mu \\text{g}.$ Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{M(t)}{M(0)} & =\\frac{0.5\\,\\mu \\text{g}}{8.0\\,\\mu \\text{g}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16} \\\\  & ={\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{4},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\nM(t)={\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{4}\\cdot M(0).\n$$\n\nFrom this equation, we see that $4$ half-lives must have occurred for the mass to reduce from $8.0\\,\\mu \\text{g}$ to $0.5\\,\\mu \\text{g}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\nt=4\\cdot {t}_{1/2}=4\\cdot 5.27=21.08\\,\\text{yr}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321139 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "98827adc-2ae4-5e58-89ee-13340a8d90f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "548.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319842}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "98827adc-2ae4-5e58-89ee-13340a8d90f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "548.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319842} :answer-field/id #uuid "31423f8a-cf58-5641-ab59-0f4d5ca498ee" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320278}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2c2b0789-a049-58b0-a4c0-cb60d52adde7" :question/math-academy-id "q-353523" :question/problem "The half-life of fluorine-18 is $109.7$ minutes. How long does it take for a $6.4\\,\\text{ng}$ sample of fluorine-18 to reduce to $0.2\\,\\text{ng}?$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$t=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{min}$" :question/worked-solution "The mass $M(t)$ of a radioactive substance at time $t$ can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n{M}^{′}(t)=-kM(t),\\,M(0)={M}_{0}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-kt}.\n$$\n\nThe half-life ${t}_{1/2}$ is the time taken for the mass of the substance to reduce by one half and is given by\n\n$$\n{t}_{1/2}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}.\n$$\n\nThere are two methods that we can use to solve this problem. We describe each of them below.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nSince we know that ${t}_{1/2}=109.7$ minutes, we have\n\n$$\n109.7=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}\\,⇒\\,k=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{109.7}\n$$\n\nAlso, since ${M}_{0}=6.4\\,\\text{ng},$ we have the particular solution\n\n$$\nM(t)=6.4{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/109.7}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to calculate the time $t$ such that $M(t)=0.2\\,\\text{ng}.$ So, we set up our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.2 & =6.4{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/109.7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.2 & =6.4{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/109.7} \\\\ \\frac{0.2}{6.4} & ={e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/109.7} \\\\ \\frac{1}{32} & ={e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/109.7} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{32}\\right) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/109.7}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡32 & =-\\frac{t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{109.7} \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡32 & =-\\frac{t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{109.7} \\\\ t & =\\frac{109.7\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡32}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ t & =\\frac{109.7\\cdot 5\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ t & =548.5\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe wish to calculate the time $t$ such that $M(t)=0.2\\,\\text{ng}.$ Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{M(t)}{M(0)} & =\\frac{0.2\\,\\text{ng}}{6.4\\,\\text{ng}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{32} \\\\  & ={\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{5},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\nM(t)={\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{5}\\cdot M(0).\n$$\n\nFrom this equation, we see that $5$ half-lives must have occurred for the mass to reduce from $6.4\\,\\text{ng}$ to $0.2\\,\\text{ng}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\nt=5\\cdot {t}_{1/2}=5\\cdot 109.7=548.5\\,\\text{min}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321140 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f1b56a7-f6ec-58b3-a41e-b0232d847e44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "415.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319537}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2f1b56a7-f6ec-58b3-a41e-b0232d847e44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "415.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319537} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ba55605-f61b-5f9b-8ee0-6f8d137abe0a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320323}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d67921e3-14ad-5197-b390-1f8f4c638645" :question/math-academy-id "q-353546" :question/problem "The half-life of polonium-210 is $138.4$ days. How long does it take for a $8.8\\,\\mu \\text{g}$ sample of polonium-210 to reduce to $1.1\\,\\mu \\text{g}?$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$t=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{days}$" :question/worked-solution "The mass $M(t)$ of a radioactive substance at time $t$ can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n{M}^{′}(t)=-kM(t),\\,M(0)={M}_{0}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-kt}.\n$$\n\nThe half-life ${t}_{1/2}$ is the time taken for the mass of the substance to reduce by one half and is given by\n\n$$\n{t}_{1/2}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}.\n$$\n\nThere are two methods that we can use to solve this problem. We describe each of them below.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nSince we know that ${t}_{1/2}=138.4$ days, we have\n\n$$\n138.4=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}\\,⇒\\,k=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{138.4}\n$$\n\nAlso, since ${M}_{0}=8.8\\,\\mu \\text{g},$ we have the particular solution\n\n$$\nM(t)=8.8{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/138.4}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to calculate the time $t$ such that $M(t)=1.1\\,\\mu \\text{g}.$ So, we set up our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1.1 & =8.8{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/138.4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1.1 & =8.8{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/138.4} \\\\ \\frac{1.1}{8.8} & ={e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/138.4} \\\\ \\frac{1}{8} & ={e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/138.4} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{8}\\right) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/138.4}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡8 & =-\\frac{t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{138.4} \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡8 & =-\\frac{t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{138.4} \\\\ t & =\\frac{138.4\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡8}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ t & =\\frac{138.4\\cdot 3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ t & =415.2\\,\\text{days}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe wish to calculate the time $t$ such that $M(t)=1.1\\,\\mu \\text{g}.$ Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{M(t)}{M(0)} & =\\frac{1.1\\,\\mu \\text{g}}{8.8\\,\\mu \\text{g}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8} \\\\  & ={\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\nM(t)={\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{3}\\cdot M(0).\n$$\n\nFrom this equation, we see that $3$ half-lives must have occurred for the mass to reduce from $8.8\\,\\mu \\text{g}$ to $1.1\\,\\mu \\text{g}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\nt=3\\cdot {t}_{1/2}=3\\cdot 138.4=415.2\\,\\text{days}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321144 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "98f64674-1995-556d-a62a-5d5e213cfa49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319847}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "98f64674-1995-556d-a62a-5d5e213cfa49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319847} :answer-field/id #uuid "34b5dd46-1f84-5f95-95f4-aab4a6c2e8ba" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320290}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f6f4f6ad-6fb0-5c26-b7f6-1c819ea19862" :question/math-academy-id "q-353610" :question/problem "In a medical imaging facility, a tracer is found with $25\\%$ of its original sodium-22 content. Given that the half-life of sodium-22 is $2.6$ years, how old is the tracer? Round your answer to **one decimal place**.\n\nThe tracer is approximately\n{{field-1}}\nyears old." :question/worked-solution "The mass $M(t)$ of a radioactive substance at time $t$ can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n{M}^{′}(t)=-kM(t),\\,M(0)={M}_{0}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-kt}.\n$$\n\nThe half-life ${t}_{1/2}$ is the time taken for the mass of the substance to reduce by one half and is given by\n\n$$\n{t}_{1/2}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}.\n$$\n\nSince we know that ${t}_{1/2}=2.6$ years, we have\n\n$$\n2.6=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}\\,⇒\\,k=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{2.6}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/2.6}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to calculate the time $t$ such that $M(t)=0.25{M}_{0}.$ So, we set up our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n0.25{M}_{0}={M}_{0}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/2.6}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.25{M}_{0} & ={M}_{0}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/2.6} \\\\ 0.25\\cancel{{M}_{0}} & =\\cancel{{M}_{0}}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/2.6} \\\\ 0.25 & ={e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/2.6} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.25) & =ln\\,({e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/2.6}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.25) & =-\\frac{t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{2.6} \\\\ t & =-\\frac{2.6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.25)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ t & ≈5.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the tracer is approximately $5.2$ years old."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323697 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ec931fec-8d82-555a-86a2-d57c3ecbd278" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0403" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323009}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ec931fec-8d82-555a-86a2-d57c3ecbd278" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0403" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323009} :answer-field/id #uuid "b3699b56-4089-5435-9d3a-86b8cdd70326" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323518}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "bc4688bc-7b3a-50f3-917e-c03d9c84742f" :question/math-academy-id "q-355590" :question/problem "The mass $M(t),$ in micrograms, of a sample of neon-19 is given by\n\n$$\nM(t)=4.5{e}^{-kt},\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in seconds. The half-life of neon-19 is $17.2\\,\\text{s}.$ Rounded to **three significant figures**, what is the value of $k?$\n\n$k≈$\n{{field-1}} ${\\text{s}}^{-1}$" :question/worked-solution "The mass $M(t)$ of a radioactive substance at time $t$ can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n{M}^{′}(t)=-kM(t),\\,M(0)={M}_{0}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-kt}.\n$$\n\nThe half-life ${t}_{1/2}$ is the time taken for the mass of the substance to reduce by one half and is given by\n\n$$\n{t}_{1/2}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}.\n$$\n\nSince we know that ${t}_{1/2}=17.2\\,\\text{s},$ we have\n\n$$\n17.2=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}\\,⇒\\,k=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{17.2}≈0.0403\\,{\\text{s}}^{-1}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323699 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "84a5680f-cb88-5101-ac0e-2b844681eede" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322194}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "84a5680f-cb88-5101-ac0e-2b844681eede" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322194} :answer-field/id #uuid "a248fbc4-d9f5-5dc9-a96b-f8e0a40fc069" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323484}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "bcdbbdf2-e529-5100-b47e-737a1544e182" :question/math-academy-id "q-355651" :question/problem "At a deep-sea sediment site, a sample is found with $30\\%$ of its original beryllium-10 content. Given that the half-life of beryllium-10 is $1.39$ million years, how old is the sample? Round your answer to **one decimal place**.\n\nThe sample is approximately\n{{field-1}}\nmillion years old." :question/worked-solution "The mass $M(t)$ of a radioactive substance at time $t$ can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n{M}^{′}(t)=-kM(t),\\,M(0)={M}_{0}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-kt}.\n$$\n\nThe half-life ${t}_{1/2}$ is the time taken for the mass of the substance to reduce by one half and is given by\n\n$$\n{t}_{1/2}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}.\n$$\n\nSince we know that ${t}_{1/2}=1.39$ million years, we have\n\n$$\n1.39=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}\\,⇒\\,k=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{1.39}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/1.39}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to calculate the time $t$ such that $M(t)=0.30{M}_{0}.$ So, we set up our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n0.30{M}_{0}={M}_{0}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/1.39}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.30{M}_{0} & ={M}_{0}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/1.39} \\\\ 0.30\\cancel{{M}_{0}} & =\\cancel{{M}_{0}}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/1.39} \\\\ 0.30 & ={e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/1.39} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.30) & =ln\\,({e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/1.39}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.30) & =-\\frac{t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{1.39} \\\\ t & =-\\frac{1.39\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.30)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ t & ≈2.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the sample is approximately $2.4$ million years old."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323700 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c3f4ba6d-7421-50a2-b7dc-734d1de2cb0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322668}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c3f4ba6d-7421-50a2-b7dc-734d1de2cb0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322668} :answer-field/id #uuid "e78f0cb6-4d3d-5a65-9d77-924edacc3a53" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323625}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "abe0dbf1-e6be-5b2c-8252-d7609fb70ece" :question/math-academy-id "q-355652" :question/problem "At a geologic study site, a sample is found with $42\\%$ of its original cesium-135 content. Given that the half-life of cesium-135 is $2.3$ million years, how old is the sample? Round your answer to **one decimal place**.\n\nThe sample is approximately\n{{field-1}}\nmillion years old." :question/worked-solution "The mass $M(t)$ of a radioactive substance at time $t$ can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n{M}^{′}(t)=-kM(t),\\,M(0)={M}_{0}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-kt}.\n$$\n\nThe half-life ${t}_{1/2}$ is the time taken for the mass of the substance to reduce by one half and is given by\n\n$$\n{t}_{1/2}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}.\n$$\n\nSince we know that ${t}_{1/2}=2.3$ million years, we have\n\n$$\n2.3=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}\\,⇒\\,k=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{2.3}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/2.3}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to calculate the time $t$ such that $M(t)=0.42{M}_{0}.$ So, we set up our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n0.42{M}_{0}={M}_{0}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/2.3}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.42{M}_{0} & ={M}_{0}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/2.3} \\\\ 0.42\\cancel{{M}_{0}} & =\\cancel{{M}_{0}}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/2.3} \\\\ 0.42 & ={e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/2.3} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.42) & =ln\\,({e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/2.3}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.42) & =-\\frac{t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{2.3} \\\\ t & =-\\frac{2.3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.42)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ t & ≈2.9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the sample is approximately $2.9$ million years old."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323732 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09053cf6-741f-589d-be34-d2a1b6174a2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321218} {:answer/id #uuid "4518ed82-7e07-5dd3-ac80-6f7f294b2a4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321695} {:answer/id #uuid "4c778daa-2aae-56ec-994c-19cb5cc2cae5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321748} {:answer/id #uuid "76d49207-26c4-56ac-a485-a6f407cdb31d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322072} {:answer/id #uuid "88032f36-3eb2-5a47-a909-5e2c7d37b4a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322230}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "76d49207-26c4-56ac-a485-a6f407cdb31d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322072} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f544854-0876-5f69-aa6b-c91df359ae81" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323294}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "6c3de3bc-c5ed-54c9-b107-f7edd673c56e" :question/math-academy-id "q-35956" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{(x,y,z)\\to (1,-1,1)}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+xy-{y}^{2}}{z{x}^{2}-z{y}^{2}}=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Unfortunately, this limit takes the indeterminate form because the numerator and denominator both evaluate to zero at the given point:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{(x,y,z)\\to (1,-1,1)}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+xy-{y}^{2}}{z{x}^{2}-z{y}^{2}}=\\frac{2\\cdot {1}^{2}+1\\cdot (-1)-(-1)^{2}}{1\\cdot {1}^{2}-1\\cdot (-1)^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{(x,y,z)\\to (1,-1,1)}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+xy-{y}^{2}}{z{x}^{2}-z{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{(x,y,z)\\to (1,-1,1)}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+2xy-xy-{y}^{2}}{z({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{(x,y,z)\\to (1,-1,1)}\\frac{2x(x+y)-y(x+y)}{z(x+y)(x-y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{(x,y,z)\\to (1,-1,1)}\\frac{(2x-y)(x+y)}{z(x+y)(x-y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{(x,y,z)\\to (1,-1,1)}\\frac{2x-y}{z(x-y)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{(x,y,z)\\to (1,-1,1)}\\frac{2x-y}{z(x-y)} & =\\frac{2(1)-(-1)}{1(1-(-1))} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $\\frac{3}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323752 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5003a38c-04a4-5836-8966-aa51b40a5742" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321774} {:answer/id #uuid "9a78d252-aa58-5541-9491-1c5625863789" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322373} {:answer/id #uuid "a56d86fa-d11f-5d28-87f6-a135179a191b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322445} {:answer/id #uuid "e2ebe706-b739-54ea-a7f7-8097ef2e52ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322922} {:answer/id #uuid "e2f20116-344c-5a96-91a8-63d897848e44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322923}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e2ebe706-b739-54ea-a7f7-8097ef2e52ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322922} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8202b9f-8756-5c9e-8ce9-e8e73b40cad5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323626}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "bb26a78b-1c42-5b21-af43-2a1b185bc53e" :question/math-academy-id "q-36108" :question/problem "$\\lim_{x\\to (-1,1,0)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+yz+{z}^{2}}{{x}^{2}-xy+xz-yz}=$" :question/worked-solution "The function \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+yz+{z}^{2}}{{x}^{2}-xy+xz-yz}\n$$\n\nis continuous except when the denominator is zero. However, the point $(-1,1,0)$ does not cause the denominator to become zero, so we can calculate the limit by substituting  $(-1,1,0)$ into the function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (-1,1,0)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+yz+{z}^{2}}{{x}^{2}-xy+xz-yz} & =\\frac{(-1)^{2}+(1)(0)+(0)^{2}}{(-1)^{2}-(-1)(1)+(-1)(0)-(1)(0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+0+0}{1+1+0-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323753 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5493b42d-db22-5575-bb2f-f6c8fc5d6eaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321811} {:answer/id #uuid "558ecdfc-8fe6-5e98-a11d-9431a5f8ca70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321824} {:answer/id #uuid "db11aabe-6fb7-5f73-bda7-0a282cd8baf5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322863} {:answer/id #uuid "f53896e5-4b71-5c33-b0fd-046da56c652b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323070} {:answer/id #uuid "fdbdb260-48f2-5b5b-b5cd-96873b9883f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323148}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5493b42d-db22-5575-bb2f-f6c8fc5d6eaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321811} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c09b5db-1f6a-58bf-8c80-c3937e3dcaaf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323311}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "9ae648fa-9940-58c2-8b0b-e34933c46f7d" :question/math-academy-id "q-36109" :question/problem "$$\n\\lim_{(x,y)\\to (1,2)}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+3xy-2{y}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-xy}=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\lim_{(x,y)\\to (1,2)}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+3xy-2{y}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-xy}=\\frac{2\\cdot {1}^{2}+3\\cdot 1\\cdot 2-2\\cdot {2}^{2}}{2\\cdot {1}^{2}-1\\cdot 2}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first factoring the denominator, and then canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{(x,y)\\to (1,2)}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+3xy-2{y}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-xy} & =\\lim_{(x,y)\\to (1,2)}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+4xy-xy-2{y}^{2}}{x(2x-y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{(x,y)\\to (1,2)}\\frac{2x(x+2y)-y(x+2y)}{x(2x-y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{(x,y)\\to (1,2)}\\frac{(2x-y)(x+2y)}{x(2x-y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{(x,y)\\to (1,2)}\\frac{x+2y}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{(x,y)\\to (1,2)}\\frac{x+2y}{x} & =\\frac{1+2\\cdot 2}{1} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323783 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a7048b4f-eab9-53d1-89fb-1d65b5316873" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{e}^{4}-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322455}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a7048b4f-eab9-53d1-89fb-1d65b5316873" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{e}^{4}-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322455} :answer-field/id #uuid "efb3e809-f5f1-5a7d-8ffc-3c402b2e5aaa" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323641}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "fd07d931-c8c4-5e1c-9858-8aed94aa2f51" :question/math-academy-id "q-362689" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-1}^{0}12{e}^{-4x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$  \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If we let $u=-4x,$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-4\\,⟹\\,-\\frac{1}{4}\\text{d}u=\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nComputing our limits of integration, we get the following:\n\n| $x$ | $-1$ | $0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $4$ | $0$ |\n\nThen, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}12{e}^{-4x}\\,\\text{d}x & =12{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{e}^{-4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =12{\\int }_{4}^{0}{e}^{u}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{4}\\right)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-3{\\int }_{4}^{0}{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-3\\cdot {e}^{u}\\,{|}_{4}^{0} \\\\  & =-3({e}^{0}-{e}^{4}) \\\\  & =-3(1-{e}^{4}) \\\\  & =3{e}^{4}-3\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329890 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d41e3a6-5106-5f1f-89a7-bdf0659fd667" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327605} {:answer/id #uuid "5722ae40-633d-5817-9b2f-fd696d396624" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327690} {:answer/id #uuid "7d059a2c-54e1-581a-9631-274cba73cd9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328010} {:answer/id #uuid "e54916ae-cb46-54d5-9769-ffc8af47b71e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328941} {:answer/id #uuid "ef1b3eab-bf60-5779-ac14-8316a78be3d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329022}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5722ae40-633d-5817-9b2f-fd696d396624" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327690} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b4c9edf-2978-575c-9b65-ca2b012c2b2f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329326}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "33d86109-fb1d-5bea-8ffd-bc18d4e7651a" :question/math-academy-id "q-48515" :question/problem "If a curve $C$ is given by $y=(3x-2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=1.$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate using the product rule. Let $u(x)=3x-2$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$\n\nApplying the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =(3x-2)\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}+(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =\\frac{3x-2}{x}+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=1$ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=1} & ={(\\frac{3x-2}{x}+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)|}_{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(1)-2}{1}+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $1.$"}]}
